You can use the bitwise AND operator (`&`) with `1`. An integer `n` is even if `(n & 1)` evaluates to `0`, and it's odd if `(n & 1)` evaluates to `1`.

This works because the least significant bit (LSB) of any binary number is `1` for odd numbers and `0` for even numbers. Performing a bitwise AND with `1` isolates the LSB.

```c
int n = 7;
if (n & 1) {
    // n is odd
}
// Or for even:
int m = 4;
if (!(m & 1)) {
    // m is even
}
```
